# ORACLE SQL INTERVIEW QUESTIONS – MCQ

## 1. What will be the output of the following query in Oracle?

```sql
WITH Numbers AS (
    SELECT 1 AS n FROM DUAL
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 2 FROM DUAL
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 3 FROM DUAL
    UNION ALL
    SELECT 4 FROM DUAL
)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total
FROM Numbers A
JOIN Numbers B ON A.n < B.n;
```

A. 3
B. 4
C. 6
D. 12

## 2. No GROUP BY – Will HAVING still work?

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*) AS Total
FROM Employees
HAVING COUNT(*) > 100;
```

Employees table contains 10 rows. What is the output?

A. 0
B. 10
C. Empty result set
D. Query fails because HAVING requires GROUP BY

## 3. Two employees have the same salary. You use ROW_NUMBER() to rank them by salary. After deployment, their row numbers switch. Why can this happen, and how would you make the result deterministic?

A. ROW_NUMBER() always generates random numbers.
B. The ORDER BY column contains duplicate values; add a unique tie-breaker column.
C. PARTITION BY must always contain the employee ID.
D. ROW_NUMBER() does not support salary columns.

## 4. Find the error in the following Oracle SQL query.

```sql
SELECT order_id,
       quantity * price AS total
FROM order_items
WHERE total > 1000;
```

A. Multiplication is not allowed in SELECT.
B. Column aliases cannot be referenced in the WHERE clause at the same query level.
C. FROM clause must come after WHERE.
D. The query is completely valid in Oracle.

## 5. Find the issue in the following SQL query.

```sql
SELECT c.customer_id,
       SUM(o.amount) AS total_spent
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o
    ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
JOIN order_items oi
    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id
GROUP BY c.customer_id;
```

Assume each order can contain multiple order items. What is the most likely issue?

A. GROUP BY cannot be used with SUM().
B. Joining order_items can multiply order-level amounts and inflate SUM(o.amount).
C. INNER JOIN does not support multiple tables.
D. SUM() cannot work with decimal values.

## 6. A query uses an indexed date column but still performs a full table scan. Which of the following could explain this?

